  "content": "{\n  \"findings\": {\n    \"answer\": \"E[N_R]=R/2 and E[exp(-lambda N_R)]=(1+exp(-lambda R))/2.\",\n    \"justification\": \"Since N_R equals 0 and R with probability 1/2 each, E[N_R]=(0+R)/2=R/2. Likewise, E[exp(-lambda N_R)]=(1+exp(-lambda R))/2, which is at least 1/2. Therefore a bound E[exp(-lambda N_R)]<=C/R would require C>=R/2 for every R, impossible for a constant C independent of R. Thus a large mean alone cannot establish that bound.\"\n  },\n  \"claim_status\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n  \"first_missing_step\": null,\n  \"unexpected_findings\": []\n}",
